import { S } from "../../lib/state";
import Article from "../../layouts/Article.astro";
---
<Article
  title="How much does water damage restoration cost?"
  seoTitle="Water Damage Restoration Cost in 2026: Ranges by Category and What Drives the Price"
  description="Typical 2026 price ranges for water damage restoration, mold remediation and fire and smoke cleanup, what drives the cost up or down, and how insurance and deductibles fit in."
  eyebrow="Guide · Cost"
  dek="Most water damage jobs land in the low thousands, but the range is wide. Here's what the national cost guides show and the factors that move your bill."
  path="/guides/water-damage-restoration-cost/"
  crumbs={[{ name: "Guides", href: "/guides/" }, { name: "Cost", href: "/guides/water-damage-restoration-cost/" }]}
  related={[
    { href: "/tools/cost-estimator/", label: "Estimate your job" },
    { href: "/guides/water-damage-categories/", label: "Water categories explained" },
    { href: `/guides/${S.slug}-water-damage-insurance-claim/`, label: "Insurance and deductibles" },
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: "LawnStarter: How much does water damage restoration cost in 2026?", href: "https://www.lawnstarter.com/blog/?p=75887" },
    { label: "LawnStarter: Fire and smoke damage restoration cost in 2026", href: "https://www.lawnstarter.com/blog/cost/fire-and-smoke-restoration-price" },
    { label: "HomeAdvisor: Fire and smoke damage repair cost", href: "https://www.homeadvisor.com/cost/disaster-recovery/repair-fire-and-smoke-damage" },
  ]}
>
  <p class="notice notice-amber">These are national ranges from published 2026 cost guides, not quotes. {S.name} prices vary by town, by contractor, and above all by how much is wet and how dirty the water is. Get a written estimate.</p>

  <h2>Typical ranges</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Common range</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Water mitigation, Category 1 (clean)</td><td>About $3 to $4 per sq ft</td><td>Extraction and drying. Least expensive if started within a day.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water mitigation, Category 2 (gray)</td><td>About $4 to $6.50 per sq ft</td><td>Adds antimicrobial treatment. Pad and some drywall usually replaced.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water mitigation, Category 3 (black)</td><td>About $7 per sq ft and up</td><td>Protective equipment, removal of porous materials, sanitizing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Whole water damage job</td><td>About $1,300 to $6,400, averaging near $3,900</td><td>Mitigation plus typical repairs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mold remediation</td><td>About $500 to $12,000, averaging near $3,000</td><td>Small bathroom jobs at the low end; whole-house jobs far higher.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fire and smoke restoration</td><td>About $2,900 to $38,000, averaging near $20,000</td><td>Driven by how far smoke and soot traveled, and water from firefighting.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What pushes the price up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Category of water.</strong> Contaminated water means more removal and more cleaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Time.</strong> Every day of waiting lets water wick higher and adds the risk of mold.</li>
    <li><strong>Materials.</strong> Hardwood, plaster and finished basements take longer to dry and cost more to replace.</li>
    <li><strong>Access.</strong> Water inside walls, under cabinets or in crawl spaces needs more demolition to reach.</li>
    <li><strong>After-hours work.</strong> Nights, weekends and holidays can cost more.</li>
    <li><strong>Rebuild.</strong> Mitigation (drying) and reconstruction (new drywall, paint, flooring) are often billed separately.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What pushes it down</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Shutting off the water quickly and starting drying the same day.</li>
    <li>Moving belongings out of wet rooms yourself.</li>
    <li>Choosing a company that dries in place with moisture monitoring, instead of removing materials by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How insurance changes the math</h2>
  <p>If the cause is covered, you usually pay your deductible and the insurer pays the rest of the approved scope. Many restoration companies bill the insurer directly using estimating software the insurer also uses. Ask before work starts what's covered, what your deductible is, and whether anything will be billed to you separately. If the cause isn't covered, ask for an itemized estimate and compare at least two.</p>
</Article>
